This directory contains the build variant and the bundled config.json for InterCom,
an unofficial, modified build of Element Desktop / Element Web.

InterCom is not affiliated with, endorsed by or supported by Element (New Vector Ltd).
"Element" is a trademark of New Vector Ltd and is deliberately not used in the name,
icon or branding of this build. The upstream licence and copyright notices are kept.

build.json electron-builder variant (app id, executable name, protocols)
config.json config baked into webapp.asar (brand, theme, no Element services)
icon.svg source of every icon; regenerate the rasters with gen-icons.sh

Building (Linux)
----------------

intercom/build.sh web app + webapp.asar + dist/linux-unpacked and a tar.gz
intercom/build.sh --skip-web reuse the existing apps/web/webapp
Set WEBPACK_JOBS=<n> to cap the minifier's worker processes when memory is tight.

INTERCOM_DIST_DIR=dist-next intercom/build.sh    build beside dist/ while the app in dist/ is running
INTERCOM_TARGET=win intercom/build.sh            Windows x64 installer into dist-win/, from Linux (see below)

Profile and environment
-----------------------

The profile lives in ~/.config/InterCom (never ~/.config/Element). INTERCOM_PROFILE_DIR and
INTERCOM_DESKTOP_CONFIG_JSON replace Element's ELEMENT_PROFILE_DIR / ELEMENT_DESKTOP_CONFIG_JSON,
and a ~/.config/InterCom/config.json overrides the bundled config.json (homeserver, theme, ...).

Hardware video on Linux (Electron 44 / Chromium 152)
----------------------------------------------------

Enabled by default (src/intercom.ts): AcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxGL, AcceleratedVideoEncoder,
VaapiOnNvidiaGPUs, VaapiIgnoreDriverChecks. Opt out with --no-hw-video or INTERCOM_NO_HW_VIDEO=1;
--hw-video-zero-copy additionally enables AcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxZeroCopyGL.

- Decode goes through VA-API. With NVIDIA this needs nvidia-vaapi-driver, and VaapiOnNvidiaGPUs
  is what makes Chromium accept it.
- Encode needs a VA driver with encode entrypoints (Intel, AMD). nvidia-vaapi-driver only
  decodes and Chromium has no NVENC backend on Linux, so on NVIDIA calls and screen shares are
  encoded in software whatever the flags.
- The Wayland screen capturer is PipeWire through xdg-desktop-portal by default; there is no
  flag for it any more.

Audio
-----

- WebRtcAllowInputVolumeAdjustment is disabled, so calls never move the system capture volume.
- Chromium does not list PulseAudio/PipeWire _monitor_ sources as microphones. To feed a sink
  into a call, expose it as a real source, e.g.
  pactl load-module module-remap-source master=<sink>.monitor source_name=<name>

Diagnostics
-----------

Every run writes <profile>/logs/diag-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.jsonl (~/.config/InterCom/logs on Linux,
%APPDATA%\InterCom\logs on Windows): one JSON record per line, 200 MB in total, oldest deleted
first. It holds
- an app_start record (versions, OS, Wayland/X11, feature switches, GPU feature status),
- every console line of every frame of every window that starts with "[intercom-diag] ", which
  is how Element Call reports the statistics of every track every 2 s and what happens in a call
  (src/intercom-diagnostics.ts here, src/diagnostics/ in element-call),
- console warnings and errors of all frames, with anything that looks like a token taken out.
Frame snapshots taken from the call's diagnostics overlay land in logs/snapshots/.
Help > "Open diagnostics folder" (also in the tray menu) opens the folder; Ctrl+Shift+D in a call
toggles the overlay; ../../../../tools/diag-summary.py turns one or more logs into verdicts.

Windows build from Linux
------------------------

INTERCOM_TARGET=win intercom/build.sh --skip-web builds dist-win/InterCom Setup <version>.exe
(NSIS, x64) with wine. It is not code signed, so SmartScreen warns on first start. The seshat
search index is included when its Windows prebuilt (@matrix-org/seshat-win32-x64-msvc) is
installed; without it the app runs but cannot search encrypted rooms.
